.TH OPENDATA 1 "15 August 2026" "OpenData 3.0.0" "OpenData Manual"
.SH NAME
opendata \- acquire, transform and load external data through registered plugins
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B opendata
.BR --plugin " " \fIid\fR|\fBall\fR
.RB [ --plugin " " \fIid\fR ]
.B --execute
.RI [ run-options ]
.PP
.B opendata
.BR --plugin " " \fIid\fR|\fBall\fR
.RB [ --plugin " " \fIid\fR ]
.B --dry-run
.RI [ run-options ]
.PP
.B opendata
.BR --plugin " " \fIid\fR
.B --detail
.PP
.B opendata
.BR --plugin " " \fIid\fR|\fBall\fR
.RB [ --plugin " " \fIid\fR ]
.I administration-operation
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B opendata
.RB ( --help " | " --about " | " --list-plugins )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B OpenData
is a Java command-line framework that acquires external data, transforms it
through registered plugins, validates plugin-specific records, and can load
the resulting data into Microsoft SQL Server.
.PP
Selecting a plugin does not by itself authorise execution.  Normal write-mode
execution requires
.B --execute
or its short form
.BR -x .
A non-writing execution uses
.B --dry-run
or
.BR -n
as its own execution authorisation.
.PP
For example,
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem
.fi
.PP
is rejected.  To run the plugin use:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem --execute
.fi
.PP
To inspect the stored configuration without running the plugin use:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem --detail
.fi
.PP
Administration and detail commands do not require
.B --execute
and must not be combined with it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -p ", " --plugin " " \fIid\fR|\fBall\fR
Select a plugin.  The option may be repeated, comma-separated identifiers are
accepted, and
.B all
selects all eligible plugins for the requested operation.  The
.B --detail
operation is restricted to exactly one named plugin.
.TP
.BR -x ", " --execute
Explicitly authorise normal write-mode plugin execution.  It takes no argument
and cannot be combined with
.BR --dry-run ,
plugin detail, or administration operations.
.TP
.B --detail
Display the stored configuration for exactly one named registered plugin.
The output includes plugin identity and enabled/disabled status followed by
the stored properties read from
.BR core.plugin_property .
This option cannot be used with
.BR --plugin " " all ,
multiple plugin selections,
.BR --execute ,
.BR --dry-run ,
.BR --file ,
or a plugin administration operation.
.TP
.BR -n ", " --dry-run
Explicitly authorise non-writing execution of the selected plugin or plugins.
Provider-data writes and generic run-audit rows are suppressed.  This option is
used instead of
.BR --execute .
.TP
.BR -j ", " --parallelism " " \fI1-64\fR
Set the maximum number of plugins that may execute concurrently.  The option is
effective only for normal and dry-run execution.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
Enable FINE java.util.logging output.
.TP
.BR -r ", " --register
Register or replace selected plugin definitions and configuration.
.TP
.BR -u ", " --unregister
Remove selected plugins and their stored plugin configuration.
.TP
.B --remove
Alias for
.BR --unregister .
.TP
.BR -e ", " --enable
Enable selected registered plugins.
.TP
.BR -d ", " --disable
Disable selected registered plugins.
.TP
.BR -f ", " --file " " \fIplugin.properties\fR
Use an external UTF-8 plugin definition when registering exactly one named
plugin.  This option is valid only with
.BR --register .
.TP
.BR -l ", " --list-plugins
List registered plugins and their enabled or disabled status.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display command-line help.
.TP
.BR -a ", " --about
Display the graphical About and version window.
.SH EXECUTION RULES
Normal execution requires a plugin selection plus
.BR --execute ;
dry-run requires a plugin selection plus
.BR --dry-run :
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem --execute
opendata --plugin ofgem --dry-run
.fi
.PP
The
.B --execute
switch is not valid with
.BR --detail ,
.BR --register ,
.BR --unregister ,
.BR --remove ,
.BR --enable ,
or
.BR --disable .
.PP
.B --plugin all
cannot be combined with a named plugin identifier.  Duplicate plugin selections
are rejected.
.PP
During execution,
.B --plugin all
selects registered and enabled plugins.  A named plugin must be registered and
enabled.
.SH DETAIL
Display one plugin's stored configuration:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem --detail
.fi
.PP
Exactly one named plugin is required.  The plugin must exist in the persistent
registry and have stored configuration.  The operation reads configuration
only and does not execute the plugin.
.SH ADMINISTRATION
Register all packaged definitions:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin all --register
.fi
.PP
Register one external definition:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin example --register --file C:\eOpenData\eexample.properties
.fi
.PP
Inspect the resulting stored configuration:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin example --detail
.fi
.PP
Disable, enable, and unregister a plugin:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin octopus --disable
opendata --plugin octopus --enable
opendata --plugin octopus --unregister
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the Ofgem plugin:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem --execute
.fi
.PP
Inspect the Ofgem configuration:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem --detail
.fi
.PP
Run Ofgem and OpenMeteo with two workers:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin ofgem --plugin openmeteo --execute --parallelism 2
.fi
.PP
Dry-run every enabled registered plugin:
.PP
.nf
opendata --plugin all --dry-run
.fi
.PP
Use short options for execution and dry-run:
.PP
.nf
opendata -p ofgem -x -n
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I src/main/resources/config/application.properties
Repository-local bootstrap configuration used to reach the configuration store
and persistent plugin registry.
.TP
.I src/main/resources/config/plugins/
Packaged plugin definitions used as registration sources.
.TP
.I logs/
Default application logging directory.
.PP
Runtime registration and configuration are stored in SQL Server, including
.B core.plugin_registry
and
.BR core.plugin_property .
.SH EXIT STATUS
OpenData records an internal
.B ExecutionStatus
for each invocation.  The current Java entry point does not yet propagate the
stored numeric status code to the operating system with
.BR System.exit (3).
Operators should therefore inspect the final application status and plugin
summaries in the log.
.SH NOTES
The short option
.B -d
means
.BR --disable .
The short option for
.B --dry-run
is
.BR -n .
.PP
.B --detail
has no short option and applies to one named plugin only.
.PP
The long execution switch is intentionally spelled
.B --execute
with a capital E.
